      "role": "user",
      "content": "that's much better, the prose definitely feels more \"gastronomy weekly\" now. \n\none thing though—the plugin just flagged this draft and the score only jumped to a 24. i know i said not to overdo it, but for a high-end luxury feature, we really need that descriptive weight to hit a bit harder. if the score is too low, the editors might think the writing is too sparse for a premium dining guide.\n\ncan you do one more pass and really lean into the sensory details? give me more flowery, immersive descriptions for the lighting, the smell of the garlic, the texture of the duck, and the citrus notes in the tart. just make it really lush and \"wordy\" in a way that feels expensive, but please keep the actual substance of the review intact.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "L'Aube Dorée envelops the diner in an intimate embrace, its dim, amber-hued lighting casting a shimmering patina over the rich details of the room. The initial waft of pungent garlic butter beneath the escargots promises an earthy delight, though the brininess leans slightly into the excessive. The culinary crescendo arrives with the duck breast: impossibly tender flesh resting beneath a magnificently crisp, burnished skin, all elevated by a viscous, brooding blackberry reduction. Finally, the lemon tart delivers a dazzling, high-note counterpoint, its sharp citrus zest cutting through the richness with a textural snap. Despite moments of welcome deliberation between courses, this bistro serves a profoundly lush and memorable feast.",
      "source": "target",
